## Introduction
Porous materials such as zeolites have been widely used industrially in many applications including gas adsorption and separation and catalysis.[1] Most porous materials are solids, and usually are susceptible to physical aging and mechanical fatigue. In contrast, porous materials in the liquid phase could avoid such problems and in addition have significant advantages in mass and heat transfer. Moreover, materials in the liquid state can be easier to control and manipulate in conventional industrial flow settings, especially in gas adsorption, separation and scrubbing. Although the concepts of porosity and fluidity may appear to be contradictory, introduction of persistent empty cavities into liquids can cause them to combine the advantages of permanent porosity and fluidity and exhibit unique properties in many applications. Since porous liquids were pioneered in 2007 by James et al., they attracted increasing attention in selective gas adsorption and separation.[2] Liquid porous materials are generally classified as three distinct types. Type I consist of rigid hosts with empty cavities that are liquid in their pure state, while types II and III are soluble hosts with intrinsic cavities and dispersed porous frameworks in sterically hindered solvents, respectively. Several synthetic strategies have been proposed for the construction of porous liquids. The simplest strategy is to disperse porous materials such as metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and zeolites in sterically hindered solvents whose molecules are too large to enter the pores of these materials.[3] Wang et al. for example fabricated the first type III porous liquid by dispersing ZIF-8 into glycol-2- methylimidazole.[3a] However, this strategy often suffers from the serious settling of empty host nanoparticles (NP) and the scarcity of solvents with large sizes. Peripheral modification of porous molecular entities or frameworks is an effective strategy to address these problems.[4] Dai et al. reported the first preparation of a type I porous liquid in a neat liquid state through surface-modification of hollow silica spheres with corona and canopy species.[4a] James et al. successfully prepared the first type II porous liquid by dispersing crown ether functionalized porous organic imine cages into a sterically bulky solvent 15- crown-5.[4b] However, only several examples of porous liquids including $S_{\mathrm{iO}_{2}}^{\mathrm{:}}$ , zeolites, carbons, porous organic cages, porous coordination cages, porous MOFs and covalent organic frameworks (COFs) have been reported to date.[3-5] This is because their cavities are easily self-filled or the porous hosts are rapidly settled in hindered solvents, making the preparation of porous liquids challenging. Thus, it is necessary to develop new strategies to fabricate porous liquids. To date, the applications of these porous liquid materials have been focused on gas separation. The investigation of other applications of porous liquids such as gas storage for catalysis is an attractive goal, which would promote the development of these materials.
As one of the most fascinating porous materials, MOFs constructed from metal ions or clusters, and organic ligands, have been widely used in a wide range of applications in gas storage, separation, catalysis or drug delivery for their unique features which include high surface areas and tunable crystalline structures.[6] However, most of the crystalline MOFs are porous solid materials, which have the same disadvantages as traditional porous solid materials. Endowing porous MOFs with fluidity could lead to unexpected properties such as low enthalpies of absorption and highly efficient heat exchange of liquid state. Due to the chemical and structural diversity of MOFs, an extensive library of porous MOFs liquids could be built. However, until now, only several MOF-based porous liquids have been prepared in the laboratory by treatment at high temperatures of special MOFs such as ZIF-4 or by dispersion of MOFs in hindered organic solvents such as bulky ionic liquids.[3ad, 5a] However, most of MOFs decompose at high temperatures, and this usually results in loss of periodic pore characteristics, precluding guest molecule binding. The approach of direct dispersion of MOFs into solvents is usually limited by the aggregation of MOF hosts and the specific solvents. Thus, it is necessary to develop new strategies for the fabrication of porous MOF liquids with intrinsic functionalities which endow them with the rich chemistry and properties that solid counterparts cannot fully possess.
In order to fabricate MOF-based porous liquids, lowering their melting points and preventing the functional groups or guest molecules from blocking the pores are the two key issues. It is well known that polyethylene glycols and imidazoliums are liquid reagents, and surface engineering of MOFs using these functionalities would liquefy MOFs, lowering their melting points.[4a, 7] In addition, coronas are generally required to prevent the fluid medium from self-filling the cavities including the intermolecular self-filling of polymeric canopies such as polyethylene glycols. Based on the above consideration, we developed an ion-exchange strategy to fabricate Im-UiO-PL, a stable porous MOF liquid, that constituted by the imidazoliumfunctionalized Deim-UiO-66 cationic framework and a negative poly(ethylene glycol)-tailed sulfonate (PEGS) canopy (Scheme 1). The selection of the imidazolium-functionalized cationic MOF Deim-UiO-66 as the crystalline ionic liquid empty host is due to its adaptability to surface engineering using an ion-exchange strategy. The long carbon chains grafted on the imidazolium groups could act as coronas to ensure that the host cavities remain empty and accessible. The PEGS canopy could deliver the Deim-UiO-66 as a liquid at room temperature. Thus, the obtained porous Im-UiO-PL can be accessible to $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecules and shows greatly enhanced $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption uptake with approximately 14 times larger than that of pure PEGS. Compared with the porous MOF solid counterparts, the unique porous liquid feature could endow Im-UiO-PL has stronger $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ storage ability and can act as a gas reservoir for the efficient cycloaddition reaction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and epoxides to produce cyclic carbonates in the atmosphere. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first report of the use of porous MOF liquid as $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ storage material for catalysis.
## Results and Discussion
Synthesis and characterization of Deim-UiO-66. The empty host Deim-UiO-66 was synthesized by a solvothermal method involving the reaction of $Z r C l_{4}$ and the imidazolium-functionalized ligand 2-[(3-decyl-imidazol-1-yl) chloride]-terephthalic acid (Deim- $\mathsf{\cdot H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ in a mixed solvent of dimethyl formamide (DMF) and acetic acid at $120^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 3 days.[8] Notably, the design of the dicarboxylate ligand Deim- $\mathsf{\cdot H_{2}B D C}$ (Scheme S1, Figure S1) was based on its capability to act as surfactant during the synthesis of Deim-UiO-66. This guarantees production of a translucent gel (Scheme 1) composed of very small $(10-20~\mathsf{n m})$ NPs, as confirmed by the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images (Figure S2). In addition, the Deim-UiO-66 NPs are stable, and can be dispersed and stored in ethanol for over half a year with no obvious aggregation phenomena. The uniform and stable Deim-UiO-66 NPs can be subsequently liquefied. The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the Deim-UiO-66 obtained in this way are consistent with the patterns calculated for UiO-66 (Figure 1a), indicating they have the same topology and that the pure sample of Deim-UiO-66 had been successfully synthesized. The structure of Deim-UiO-66 remained intact after removal of the guest molecules (Figure 1a), due to the strong coordination bonds between high-valence metal ions $Z\mathfrak{r}^{4+}$ and carboxylate linkers (776 kJ∙mol−1).[8] In order to confirm the existence of the imidazolium moiety in Deim-UiO-66, the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis was completed. As shown in the XPS spectrum in Figure S3a, the peak of N 1s at $401.6\mathsf{e V}$ could be attributed to the quaternary-N of the imidazolium groups in the frameworks. The presence of uncoordinated chloride anions was also confirmed by the peaks of Cl $2p_{1/2}$ and Cl $2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2}$ at 199.2 eV and 197.6 eV (Figure S3b), respectively. These results indicate that the imidazolium cations were not coordinated with the $Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm r}$ centers, and the free Cl- can be exchanged by the PEGS in a subsequent step. Due to the blocking/filling by the side chains, Deim-UiO-66 showed a Brunauer-Emmer-Teller (BET) surface area of $103~\mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 1b), lower than that of UiO66,[8] but consistent with the calculated value of $\mathsf{109}\mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . The pore sizes of Deim-UiO-66, calculated by Horvath-Kawazoe method (Figure 1b) are ${\sim}8\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and $11\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and are similar to those of UiO-66.[4c] The measured pore sizes for Deim-UiO-66 are larger than the theoretical calculated values of ${\sim}4\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ to 6 Å (Figure S4), and this was attributed to the defects in Deim-UiO66 and the packing of NPs. Thus, the intrinsic pores of DeimUiO-66 $(\sim4\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ to $6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , Figure S4) can be accessible to the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecule $(3.3\ \mathring\mathsf{A})$ but not to PEGS (23.1 $\mathring{\mathsf{A}},$ , Figure S5a). As shown in Figure S6, the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptakes of Deim-UiO-66 at 273, 283 and $293\mathsf{K}$ were 10.5, 8.5 and $7.2\mathsf{c m}^{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The adsorption enthalpies $(\mathsf{Q}_{\mathsf{s t}})$ at zero coverage, which represent the strength of the interactions between the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecules and the adsorbent for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ at 273, 283 and $293\mathsf{K}$ were calculated to be 22.0, 22.1 and $22.2\ k J\cdot m0!^{-1}$ , respectively. These results indicate that the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption of Deim-UiO-66 mainly depends on physical adsorption[9] and that the pores were not blocked by the imidazolium groups attached top C10-based long chain alkanes.
Synthesis and characterization of Im-UiO-PL.
The corresponding porous MOF liquid Im-UiO-PL was obtained by replacing the chloride counter anions of cationic Deim-UiO-66 with negative PEGS canopies (Scheme 1). Compared with Deim-UiO-66, additional characteristic bands of sulfonates (1205 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1},$ ) and ethers $(1100~\mathsf{c m}^{-1})$ could be clearly seen in the Fourier-transform infrared (FT-IR) spectrum of Im-UiO-PL (Figure 2a). Moreover, the absorption peak intensity at 2864 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ corresponding to the aliphatic C-H stretching mode was increased significantly. These results demonstrate that negative PEGS canopies had partially replaced the chloride anions in ImUiO-PL. In order to confirm the composition of Im-UiO-PL, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) and inductively coupled plasma-atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP) were performed. The TGA curves displayed that Im-UiO-PL could be stable up to $200~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ without obvious weight loss, indicating that no volatile solvents occupied the cavities of Deim-UiO-66 in Im-UiO-66 (Figure 2b). The final residual masses of PEGS, Deim-UiO-66 and Im-UiO-PL were $8.8\%$ , $31.8\%$ and $10.1\%$ , respectively. The weight percentage of Deim-UiO-66 framework in Im-UiO-PL was calculated as $5.7\%$ , which agrees with the ICP result (Table S1).
In a further investigation of the nanostructure, TEM and scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) of Im-UiOPL were performed. As shown in the TEM image in Figure 3a, the cationic Deim-UiO-66 frameworks are observed to be surrounded entirely by PEGS canopies. The spatial distribution of Zr and S was mapped out by the energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) and STEM experiments. As shown in the corresponding elemental mapping images (Figure 3b), Zr is distributed uniformly throughout the MOF host NPs, while S atoms are mainly distributed at the edges of the NPs. This result reveals that the cationic Deim-UiO-66 frameworks are entirely surrounded by the PEGS canopies and did not penetrate the cavities. To further demonstrate the permanent porosity of ImUiO-PL, molecular dynamics (MD) simulations were carried out. As shown in Deim-UiO-66 simulation boxes (Figures 3c-3d), pores with size of $4\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ to $6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ were observed clearly, and could be easily penetrated by $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecules (Figure S5b). The properties of PEGS were examined by density functional theory (DFT) calculations. As shown in Figure S5a, the length, width and height of PEGS in a stable state are $23.0\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , $14.7\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and 20.2 Å, respectively, showing that it is too large to enter the cavities of Deim-UiO-66. This finding is important because the empty cavities in the liquid state are still accessible to $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ gas molecules but not to PEGS.
In theory, the cationic Deim-UiO-66 frameworks could interact strongly with the negative PEGS canopies by Coulombic forces. To affirm this, the zeta potentials of Deim-UiO-66 and Im-UiO-PL dispersed in ethanol were measured. Generally, zeta potentials outside the range of $^{+50}$ to $-50~\mathsf{m V}$ are sufficient to ensure good colloidal stability, and the dispersions with low zeta potentials would eventually aggregate because of van der Waals interactions.[10] The zeta potential values of Deim-UiO-66 and Im-UiO-PL ethanol dispersions reached $102~\mathsf{m V}$ and $-115\mathsf{m V}$ , respectively (Figure S7), indicating that these systems were extremely stable. The extremely positive zeta potentials of ImUiO-PL became significantly negative after the chloride counter anions of Deim-UiO-66 were replaced by negative PEGS canopies, revealing that cationic Deim-UiO-66 frameworks had strong Coulombic force interactions with the negative PEGS canopies. In contrast, the neutral UiO-66 was utilized to fabricate a porous liquid, denoted as UiO-PL in a similar approach. The zeta potential value of UiO-PL at $33~\mathsf{m V}$ was much smaller than that of Im-UiO-PL, indicating that the neutral UiO-66 framework had insufficient interaction between PEGS canopies. The result highlights the importance of the cationic MOF frameworks via an ion-exchange strategy for fabricating porous liquids.
The glass-liquid phase transition and rheological properties of Im-UiO-PL were investigated by differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) and analysis of the oscillation-strain modulus. As shown in Figure 4a, a second-order transition at $-51\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ was observed, which corresponds to the glass transition temperature $(\mathsf{T}_{g})$ . The exothermic peak at $-6~^{\circ}C$ was assigned to the crystallization temperature $(\mathsf{T}_{c})$ . As the temperature increased, an evident firstorder transition appeared at $28^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , which corresponded to the melting temperature $(\mathsf{T}_{m})$ . As shown in DSC curve of PEGS, the corresponding $\daleth_{g}$ , $\mathsf{T}_{c}$ and ${\sf T}_{m}$ were -53, -14 and $23\circ_{\mathsf{C}}$ , respectively, lower than the corresponding values of Im-UiO-PL. This result could be attributed to the introduction of the cationic Deim-UiO-66 frameworks, which could attract negative PEGS canopies around the NPs to form locally ordered regions such as a liquid crystal. Thus, more energy and higher temperatures are necessary for Im-UiO-PL to break the ionic bonds, which indicates that the Im-UiO-PL system was more stable than the systems involving PEGS. In order to describe intuitively the fluidity of Im-UiO-PL, a vial of Im-UiO-PL was tilted briefly, and the liquid leveled off rapidly (Scheme 1, Figure S8 and Supporting Information - SI - Video 1). In a further investigation of the fluidity of Im-UiO-PL, the oscillation-strain modulus was measured. As shown in Figure 4b, the loss modulus $(\mathsf{G}")$ was always higher than the storage modulus $(G^{\prime})$ in the entire measured oscillation range, indicating that Im-UiO-PL exhibited a liquid-behavior at room temperature.
Im-UiO-PL for $\mathtt{c o}_{2}$ adsorption and catalytic conversion. Such a porous liquid material which combines permanent porosity and solvent-free fluidity may offer many opportunities for specific applications, such as gas adsorption and storage. Gravimetric gas solubility measurements of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ were performed at $298\textsf{K}$ for PEGS and Im-UiO-PL. As shown in Figure $4\mathsf{c}$ , the Im-UiO-PL showed excellent adsorption ability with a quantity of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ significantly larger than that of PEGS. The $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake of Im-UiO-PL at 9 bar was $5.93\ \mathsf{m m o l}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , which was ${\sim}14$ times larger than that of pure PEGS $(0.43\mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}g}^{-1})$ ) and much larger than that of other porous liquids such as ZIF-8-PL (1.56 mmol ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ , 10 bar),[3b] $\mathsf{H}$ -ZSM-5-liquid/[P66614][Br] (0.59 mmol ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ , 10 bar),[3e] UiO-66-liquid (1.64 mmol ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ , 10 bar),[4c] 18-C-6-PL $(0.43\mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ , 10 bar),[4d] 15-C-5-PL $(0.37\mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}g}^{-1}$ , 10 bar)[4d] and HCS-liquid $(0.57\ \mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ , 10 bar)[5c] in the similar test conditions. The high $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of ImUiO-PL indicated the porous liquid has permanent pores which provide additional free volume to store ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecules. Because the enhanced capability of adsorbing and enriching gas emanates from the presence of permanent porosity in porous liquids, most the applications of porous liquids have been focused on gas separation and storage, and use of the gas stored in porous liquids for catalysis is expected to be developed. We utilized the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ stored in Im-UiO-PL as gas source to synthesize cyclocarbonate[6e, 11] and for this purpose, we designed an experiment with two steps. Firstly, $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ was adsorbed and stored in Im-UiO-PL until the maximum uptake was reached in the autoclave with 20 bar $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . The valve of the autoclave was opened until the pressure was balanced with atmospheric pressure. In the second step, the epoxide and tetrabutyl-ammonium bromide (TBAB) were added into the autoclave to react with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ that had been stored and was released from Im-UiO-PL at $120^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in an air atmosphere.
The cycloaddition reaction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ into epichlorohydrin was chosen as a probe reaction using the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ stored in porous MOF liquid system. For a blank control experiment, only epichlorohydrin and TBAB were added into autoclave at $120^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in an air atmosphere without any adsorbents. As shown in Figure 4d and Table S2, only 3.82 mmol epichlorohydrin was transformed into chloropropene carbonate with a low yield of $19.1\%$ (Table S2, entry 1), which was ascribed to the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ remaining in the autoclave and the pipeline. When PEGS was employed as $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorbent, only $20.9\%$ yield of chloropropene carbonate was obtained and the actual amount of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorbed on PEGS was calculated to be 0.35 mmol (Figure 4d, Table S2, entry 2), which was similar to the blank experiment. This result revealed that PEGS had a weak capability to adsorb $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , as confirmed by gravimetric gas solubility measurements of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ (Figure 4c). In contrast, a high concentration of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}\left(12.1\ \mathrm{mmol}\right)$ adsorbed in Im-UiO-PL was converted to chloropropene carbonate with a high yield of $79.6\%$ (Table S2, entry 3). Such excellent activity was achieved because the Im-UiO-PL had the permanent porosity to store ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , almost 35 times greater than that of pure PEGS that was charged at 20 bar. Notably, although the porous solid MOF Deim-UiO-66 showed excellent $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption uptake capacity (Figure S9), only 3.94 mmol epichlorohydrin was converted into the corresponding product with a low yield of $19.7\%$ (Table S2, entry 4). This result was ascribed to the fact that almost all the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorbed in the cavities of the solid material Deim-UiO-66 was quickly released during the progress of decompression. In contrast to Deim-UiO-66, the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecules could be adsorbed and stored in the cavities of the porous liquid Im-UiO-PL over a longer time at room temperature. The mixture of Deim-UiO-66 and PEGS also showed a poor performance in this reaction with yield of $25.9\%$ , and the captured $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ calculated as 1.36 mmol (Table S2, entry 5), indicating that simple physical mixing of Deim-UiO-66 NPs with PEGS failed to afford a porous liquid to adsorb and store $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . The above result demonstrates that Im-UiO-PL has much better capability of enhanced ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption than that of PEGS and Deim-UiO-66, and this can be attributed to the unique property of a liquid with porosity and fluidity. From the technological point of view, it is easier to manipulate liquids, and more efficient from the perspective of mass and heat transfer. These findings could inspire practical applications in industry. For instance, porous liquids could absorb gases like $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and be subsequently pumped to a reactor for the relevant reactions.
## Conclusion
In summary, we have successfully designed and prepared ImUiO-PL, a stable porous liquid, using a facile ionization strategy by surface ionization of an imidazolium-substituted framework with a hindered poly(ethylene glycol) tailed sulfonate (PEGS) canopy. Due to the long carbon chains, which serve as coronas on the surface of Deim-UiO-66 NPs, the cavities of Deim-UiO-66 are not accessible to the PEGS but can be accessed by $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecules, and the permanent porosity is maintained in Im-UiOPL. Thus Im-UiO-PL exhibits a larger $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake capacity than that of PEGS. In addition, a high concentration of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ can be stored in cavities of Im-UiO-PL, which could be released and converted to carbonate with high yields. This is the first example of the use of a porous MOF liquid as a $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ stored reagent for catalysis. Considering the possibilities of this ionization strategy, a large library of porous liquids based on cationic materials (e.g., MOFs, covalent organic frameworks, metal-organic cages or porous organic polymers) with unexpected properties and applications could be possible.
